      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.502,
            0.069,
            0.815,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": "Introduction to Linear Polynomials"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.856,
            0.069,
            0.884,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": "19"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.108,
            0.813,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": "4. What is the coefficient of z in the polynomial $4 z ^ { 3 } + 5 z ^ { 2 } - 1 1 7$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.132,
            0.852,
            0.152
          ],
          "content": "5. What is the constant term of the polynomial $9x^{3} + 5x^{2} - 8x - 10?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.152,
            0.884,
            0.193
          ],
          "content": "Recall that polynomials of degree 1 are called **linear polynomials**. In this chapter, we shall study linear polynomials."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.216,
            0.46,
            0.239
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.2 Linear Polynomials"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.25,
            0.769,
            0.271
          ],
          "content": "We begin with some examples involving linear polynomials."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.274,
            0.884,
            0.314
          ],
          "content": "**Example 4:** The perimeter of a square of side x is 4x, which is a linear polynomial in the variable x."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.334,
            0.398,
            0.358
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.371,
            0.866,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": "Find the perimeter of squares with sides 1 cm, 1.5 cm, 2 cm, 2.5 cm and 3 cm. What will happen to the perimeters if the sides increase by 0.5 cm?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.438,
            0.885,
            0.498
          ],
          "content": "**Example 5:** A chess club charges a joining fee of \\`200 plus \\`50 for every match played. The following table shows the amount a player will have to pay as the number of matches varies."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.508,
            0.896,
            0.615
          ],
          "content": "| Number of matches played | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | … | m |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Amount paid (`) | 250 | 300 | 350 | 400 | 450 | … | 200 + 50m |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_table_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.63,
            0.886,
            0.711
          ],
          "content": "Hence, if m is the number of matches played, the total cost will be \\`(200 + 50m). Observe that 200 + 50m is a linear polynomial in the variable m. The amount paid increases by the constant value of \\`50 for every additional match played."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.734,
            0.399,
            0.758
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.771,
            0.662,
            0.793
          ],
          "content": "If a player paid \\`750, how many matches did he play?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.819,
            0.886,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": "The examples given above highlight a characteristic feature of linear polynomials — that the difference between the successive values at integers is constant. In Example 4, the perimeters increase by 2 cm each time the side of the square increases by 0.5 cm. Similarly in Example 5,"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
